.lua
book.chapter(9, "Types, inference, and unification")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Types describe permitted connections"
.pp
MetaML uses Hindley-Milner-style inference extended with numeric constraints,
row-polymorphic records, and ML-style modules. A type tells us which
connections a program may make: a string parser consumes String, an increment
function consumes a number, and a list traversal must use one consistent
element type. Inference reconstructs many of these relationships from code so
the programmer need not repeat every intermediate type annotation.
.pp
Write type variables with apostrophe-prefixed names. A signature such as
'a -> 'a says that an input type is preserved, not that a value is dynamically
untyped. At each use, the bound polymorphic function can receive a fresh
instantiation. All occurrences of a variable within that instantiation must
agree. This is how one identity binding supports several types without
allowing it to turn an integer into a string arbitrarily.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[identity :: 'a -> 'a
identity x := x
pair :: 'a -> 'b -> ('a, 'b)
pair x y := (x, y)
Test.equal "independent uses" (identity 1, identity "x") (1, "x")
Test.equal "two variables" (pair true 3) (true, 3)]=], "Polymorphism with explicit relationships")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Read compound types structurally"
.pp
Arrows associate rightward. Int -> Int -> Int is curried; (Int, Int) -> Int
accepts one tuple. A list type ['a] has one element type. A tuple ('a, 'b)
has fixed positions whose types may differ. A type-constructor application
such as Option Int or Result Int String describes a structured family.
Parenthesize a nested application when it is itself an argument, as in
Option (Option Int), so the intended grouping is visible.
.lua
book.grammar("Common type forms", {
    {"type", 'atom [ "->" type ]'},
    {"atom", '"Int" | "String" | type_variable'},
    {"atom", '"[" type "]" | "(" type "," type ")"'},
    {"atom", 'constructor type_argument*'},
    {"atom", '"{" field_types [ "|" row ] "}"'}
})
.endlua
.pp
Numeric operations introduce Num constraints rather than unconstrained
polymorphism. A sum can be generic over supported numeric inputs without
being valid for strings, functions, or arbitrary records. Numeric variables
may default in unconstrained situations. At external boundaries, choose an
explicit representation and conversion instead of relying on a printed
polymorphic-looking type to imply a native machine layout.
.sh 2 "Unification solves equations between types"
.pp
Inference generates requirements such as "the argument type of this function
must equal the type of that expression." Unification solves those requirements
by substituting variables consistently. A variable can become Int, or become
a function type, but one variable cannot simultaneously mean Int and String
within the same constraint system. Matching constructors unify their
corresponding arguments; incompatible constructors produce a type error.
.lua
book.algorithm("A conceptual unifier", {
    "Apply the current substitution to both input types.",
    "If the types are identical, return the substitution.",
    "If one side is a variable:",
    {1, "Reject a binding that would make the variable contain itself."},
    {1, "Otherwise extend the substitution with that binding."},
    "If both sides have the same constructor, unify corresponding arguments.",
    "Otherwise report the conflicting types and their source context."
})
.endlua
.pp
The self-containment check is often called the occurs check. Without it, an
equation such as a = a -> b would demand an infinite type in an ordinary
finite-type system. This is a useful example of an implementation invariant
with a direct user consequence: a failure involving self-application may
reflect an impossible type equation rather than a missing declaration. The
Type tooling module exposes selected unification operations on textual types
for experiments later in the book.
.sh 2 "Generalization has a scope"
.pp
At suitable binding boundaries, inference generalizes variables that are not
fixed by the surrounding environment. Later uses instantiate that scheme.
A function parameter is not automatically generalized independently at every
occurrence, so rank-one polymorphism is weaker than a system with arbitrary
higher-rank arguments. When a higher-order function needs several uses of a
parameter at unrelated types, consider passing separate specialized functions
or reorganizing the binding boundary.
.pp
The current implementation does not impose a general ML value restriction for
mutable opaque handles. Treat a table or parser handle as having one coherent
element type, even if a generalized binding appears permissive. Static
polymorphism does not allocate independent mutable storage for each type
instantiation. An explicit annotation or a narrow wrapper module can make the
intended homogeneous use clear to both the reader and the checker.
.sh 2 "Rows and module identities extend the picture"
.pp
A record type can specify known fields plus an open row. The type
{ name : String | r } describes records containing a string name along with
additional fields represented by r. This is static extensibility, not a
dictionary of arbitrary untyped values. Row unification must align labels and
reconcile the remaining fields, which is more involved than comparing two
fixed-position tuples. Chapter 13 develops the practical interface patterns.
.pp
Modules add named type paths and abstraction boundaries. An opaque module's
public type is not interchangeable with Int merely because its implementation
uses an integer internally. The checker must preserve the public identity
while verifying exported values against the signature. This is why module
errors are often reported separately from ordinary expression mismatches:
the failure concerns an interface promise as well as a local type equation.
.lua
book.code("metaml-error:M0301", [=[bad := 1 + "one"]=], "Incompatible operands fail before evaluation")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the type representation, substitution, inference, and unification routines
in MetaML.hpp together with metalib/cpp/Type.h. Use #type to inspect identity,
partial List.map, and a row-polymorphic projection. Before asking the tool,
write down the equations each expression imposes. Then compare your reasoning
with the inferred answer. The objective is not to memorize the checker's
variable names, which may be normalized, but to understand which relationships
remain shared and which uses receive fresh instantiations.
